NAME
       toyvm - a toy virtual machine for reverse-engineering practice (Cyber Range)

SYNOPSIS
       toyvm asm SOURCE [-o PROGRAM]
       toyvm disasm PROGRAM
       toyvm run PROGRAM [INPUT]

DESCRIPTION
       toyvm is a tiny pretend computer made up for Cyber Range - not a real
       processor. A program is a list of bytes; the machine works with a stack
       (a pile of numbers from 0 to 255: PUSH puts one on top, most
       instructions take the top ones off and put a result back).

       Reverse engineering means working out what a program does by reading
       it rather than running it. disasm ("disassemble") turns the bytes back
       into instructions you can read, so you can see what a program checks
       and what it would print.

       A program file is the line TOYVM 1 followed by its bytes in hex.

INSTRUCTIONS
       00        HALT    stop
       01 n      PUSH n  put the number n on the stack
       02        ADD     take two numbers, put back their sum (0-255)
       03        SUB     take b then a, put back a - b
       04        XOR     take two numbers, put back a XOR b
       05        OUT     take a number and print it as a character
       06        IN      put the next character of the input on the stack
                         (0 when the input has run out)
       07        DUP     copy the top number
       08 hi lo  JMP a   jump to address a (hi * 256 + lo)
       09 hi lo  JZ a    take a number; jump to a when it is 0
       0A        EQ      take two numbers, put back 1 if they are equal, else 0
       0B        DROP    throw the top number away
       0C        SWAP    swap the two top numbers

       XOR with the same number twice gives the original back, so
       PUSH x, PUSH k, XOR, OUT prints the character x XOR k - a common way to
       hide text from a quick look.

ASSEMBLY
       One instruction per line (PUSH 65, JZ fail), labels such as fail:,
       comments after ; and numbers in decimal, 0x hex or 'c'. Helpers:
       .text "abc" prints abc; .xortext KEY "abc" prints abc but keeps it
       XORed with KEY; .check KEY "abc" LABEL reads three input characters and
       jumps to LABEL unless they are abc.

EXAMPLES
           toyvm disasm checker.tvm
           toyvm run checker.tvm guess

SEE ALSO
       xorcat(1), xxd(1)
